1
00:00:01,266 --> 00:00:15,735
[SPEAKER_01]: I was thinking this whole idea of, in these times of great uncertainty, in this time of such a virus going around, these troubled times, such a difficult time, now it's time to practice.

2
00:00:15,755 --> 00:00:16,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Now it's time to practice, yeah.

3
00:00:16,916 --> 00:00:17,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Now.

4
00:00:17,556 --> 00:00:18,637
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the practice now?

5
00:00:19,278 --> 00:00:19,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

6
00:00:19,678 --> 00:00:22,080
[SPEAKER_01]: As if it was something different.

7
00:00:23,041 --> 00:00:24,542
[SPEAKER_00]: As if it was any different before, yeah.

8
00:00:24,562 --> 00:00:27,664
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when things are comfortable, I don't practice.

9
00:00:29,385 --> 00:00:33,588
[SPEAKER_01]: But when things are difficult, now you're gonna practice.

10
00:00:33,648 --> 00:00:33,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

11
00:00:34,028 --> 00:00:36,210
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when I get reminded of the practice, yeah.

12
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:40,512
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you kind of... Well, it's not really procrastination.

13
00:00:41,093 --> 00:00:41,393
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

14
00:00:41,413 --> 00:00:45,576
[SPEAKER_00]: The issue there, it's more like intoxication with the good times.

15
00:00:46,875 --> 00:00:49,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Intoxication with the health.

16
00:00:49,877 --> 00:00:56,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Intoxication with safety, with lack of risk, with lack of illnesses.

17
00:00:56,703 --> 00:01:00,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Intoxication with that whole current state of affairs.

18
00:01:00,906 --> 00:01:12,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, you don't really, like, you don't make the effort to do what you know should be done, because you kind of also know that health will not last forever.

19
00:01:14,115 --> 00:01:17,218
[SPEAKER_00]: But while you're... that's... that's what they would refer to as intoxicated.

20
00:01:17,338 --> 00:01:25,265
[SPEAKER_00]: You are intoxicated with health, with youth, with life, because you genuinely think like a drunk person, who, when he's drunk, he's invincible.

21
00:01:25,325 --> 00:01:29,028
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows it's not true, but he doesn't... he doesn't feel like it's not true.

22
00:01:29,148 --> 00:01:29,989
[SPEAKER_00]: It feels like it's true.

23
00:01:30,769 --> 00:01:33,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Like this... this is... this state... ah, great, I'll...

24
00:01:33,512 --> 00:01:34,232
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll have to seize it.

25
00:01:34,292 --> 00:01:35,233
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll make most of it.

26
00:01:36,314 --> 00:01:40,638
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, you don't actually... you lose... you voluntarily lose perspective.

27
00:01:45,030 --> 00:01:45,891
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have a choice.

28
00:01:46,071 --> 00:01:52,475
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot lose perspective there, and you can ask yourself, OK, I am healthy now, but can that change?

29
00:01:52,875 --> 00:01:53,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

30
00:01:53,855 --> 00:01:54,736
[SPEAKER_00]: The answer is immediate.

31
00:01:56,397 --> 00:01:57,958
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm alive now, but can that change?

32
00:01:58,198 --> 00:01:58,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

33
00:01:58,678 --> 00:01:59,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Another immediate answer.

34
00:01:59,919 --> 00:02:01,820
[SPEAKER_00]: But that answer requires maintenance.

35
00:02:02,141 --> 00:02:07,764
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to keep, sort of, keep keeping up with that context, because it's too easy to ignore, because it's pleasant.

36
00:02:08,144 --> 00:02:10,966
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, sorry, it's unpleasant to keep looking at it.

37
00:02:11,626 --> 00:02:12,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I could get sick.

38
00:02:13,623 --> 00:02:15,604
[SPEAKER_00]: I could get, like, you know, I could die.

39
00:02:16,144 --> 00:02:18,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, my youth, my strength is fading.

40
00:02:19,125 --> 00:02:20,825
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't measure how much, but it has to.

41
00:02:21,706 --> 00:02:27,107
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's easier to not think about it, especially because you don't see any drawback in not thinking about it.

42
00:02:28,028 --> 00:02:35,950
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we have to instruct monks and people to make the effort to start seeing the drawbacks in these things, which will then keep you in check.

43
00:02:36,631 --> 00:02:40,652
[SPEAKER_00]: But without it, without making that effort, you're not going to see the drawbacks.

44
00:02:41,278 --> 00:02:49,124
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, it's kind of... when things are difficult, you do want to practice more.

45
00:02:49,484 --> 00:02:51,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because now you see a drawback.

46
00:02:51,246 --> 00:02:53,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, now you see... Now it's thrown in your face.

47
00:02:53,407 --> 00:02:53,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

48
00:02:54,268 --> 00:03:09,099
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it's too difficult, there's no possibility of... Well... ...settling down and thinking... More often... yeah, more often than not, it's kind of too late, if you waited until, okay, now you're really sick, that's it, and now you want to practice.

49
00:03:09,559 --> 00:03:10,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's a bit...

50
00:03:10,623 --> 00:03:11,704
[SPEAKER_00]: You shouldn't have left it that far.

51
00:03:11,724 --> 00:03:22,417
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the Buddha again would tell monks to basically withdraw themselves and live in seclusion and make the effort less to regret it later on.

52
00:03:22,517 --> 00:03:25,801
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the time will come where it will be too late for the effort.

53
00:03:26,262 --> 00:03:26,822
[SPEAKER_01]: And too much.

54
00:03:27,515 --> 00:03:28,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

55
00:03:28,255 --> 00:03:28,675
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

56
00:03:28,695 --> 00:03:29,215
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too late.

57
00:03:29,475 --> 00:03:37,137
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't do anything about it, because it's just too much to bear, or simply your health is gone now, and you're just in constant pain, and so on.

58
00:03:37,157 --> 00:03:43,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to prepare beforehand, with wisdom.

59
00:03:43,578 --> 00:03:45,059
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Well, that's what wisdom is.

60
00:03:45,279 --> 00:03:46,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Foreseeing things before they occur.

61
00:03:47,239 --> 00:03:50,220
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want... you want to practice restraint.

62
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:51,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

63
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:51,620
[SPEAKER_01]: But...

64
00:03:55,558 --> 00:03:56,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Good restraint, not just...

65
00:03:57,219 --> 00:03:57,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Blind restraint.

66
00:03:58,100 --> 00:03:58,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

67
00:03:59,080 --> 00:03:59,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

68
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:00,741
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you get a bit of a pain.

69
00:04:01,241 --> 00:04:01,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It is painful.

70
00:04:02,322 --> 00:04:03,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Celibacy is painful.

71
00:04:03,343 --> 00:04:03,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

72
00:04:04,003 --> 00:04:15,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, that pain, that discomfort, will keep you on the edge, to keep you... See, that pain is in your own control.

73
00:04:16,706 --> 00:04:18,007
[SPEAKER_01]: That pain is in your own control.

74
00:04:18,127 --> 00:04:18,548
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your choice.

75
00:04:18,568 --> 00:04:23,151
[SPEAKER_00]: What the Buddha referred to is known, called as pain of renunciation.

76
00:04:23,672 --> 00:04:24,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's up to you.

77
00:04:24,953 --> 00:04:28,856
[SPEAKER_00]: You choose to renounce things on account of which you experience that pain.

78
00:04:29,417 --> 00:04:33,340
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start developing wisdom, you realize pain is not optional.

79
00:04:34,681 --> 00:04:35,582
[SPEAKER_00]: There has to be pain.

80
00:04:36,726 --> 00:04:43,530
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only choice you have, it's not like whether you're not going to have any pain, it's whether it's going to be on your own terms or not on your own terms.

81
00:04:44,391 --> 00:04:56,618
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you replace the pain of household life, as the Buddha said, as in the pain of sensuality, gain, loss, praise, blame, all these worldly wins, you would replace those pains with pains of restraint.

82
00:04:58,013 --> 00:05:02,357
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you see, ah, when I have pains of restraint, I can't have the pains of household life.

83
00:05:02,657 --> 00:05:06,500
[SPEAKER_00]: And these pains of restraint are not actually pains from the point of view of the pains of household life.

84
00:05:08,302 --> 00:05:09,182
[SPEAKER_00]: They're easy to bear.

85
00:05:09,323 --> 00:05:10,504
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not painful, actually.

86
00:05:10,564 --> 00:05:14,107
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have the same amount of pressure in needing to get rid of them.

87
00:05:14,147 --> 00:05:16,088
[SPEAKER_00]: That's if you develop wisdom on account of restraint.

88
00:05:16,208 --> 00:05:19,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, of course, restraint will be overwhelming you.

89
00:05:21,093 --> 00:05:26,337
[SPEAKER_00]: But you... it's... the only reason that it's overwhelming you is because the mind is too weak to bear it.

90
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:32,950
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind still acts out of the assumption that I must be free from pain at all cost.

91
00:05:34,793 --> 00:05:36,456
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not optional.

92
00:05:37,477 --> 00:05:39,982
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as there is this body, there will be pain arising.

93
00:05:41,790 --> 00:05:44,591
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, for as long as there is this body, there will be pleasure arising.

94
00:05:44,651 --> 00:06:06,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So then again, you replace the pleasure of the household life, pleasure of sensuality, eating whenever you want, all these things, you replace it with the pleasure of renunciation, with freedom that comes from it, with burdenlessness of it, complete lack of pressure, like genuine, like lightness, like there's absolutely nothing you do... you have to do, you don't need to do anything.

95
00:06:08,419 --> 00:06:10,520
[SPEAKER_01]: And the... the sort of...

96
00:06:12,427 --> 00:06:18,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Worldly benefit as well of doing that, say, eating once a day and choosing that.

97
00:06:19,190 --> 00:06:19,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

98
00:06:19,530 --> 00:06:22,252
[SPEAKER_01]: While you've got plenty of food, you could eat all day if you want.

99
00:06:22,412 --> 00:06:22,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

100
00:06:23,012 --> 00:06:23,772
[SPEAKER_01]: But you choose not to.

101
00:06:23,932 --> 00:06:29,475
[SPEAKER_01]: So that when there's a time of famine, when it's difficult to get food, then... Well, you'll be fine.

102
00:06:29,535 --> 00:06:34,117
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't even need to think like with some doomsday scenario at the back of your mind.

103
00:06:34,577 --> 00:06:35,277
[SPEAKER_00]: You just want to...

104
00:06:35,297 --> 00:06:35,938
[SPEAKER_00]: I have an option.

105
00:06:36,918 --> 00:06:40,740
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll either have this comfort on account of my own

106
00:06:41,466 --> 00:06:50,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Well-reasoned will and intention and choice, or I have a discomfort on account of my own unrestrained desire that controls me.

107
00:06:52,114 --> 00:06:53,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is... which pain is better?

108
00:06:53,695 --> 00:06:54,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Which pain is less risky?

109
00:06:55,816 --> 00:06:57,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Which pain is less wild?

110
00:06:58,258 --> 00:07:03,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Which pain is less likely to result in greater unpleasant repercussions?

111
00:07:04,838 --> 00:07:16,209
[SPEAKER_00]: In you saying no and restraining yourself, or in you saying yes and riding that wild desire for wherever it takes you, scratching your itch like a maniac, which one do you think is going to result in worse states of mind?

112
00:07:16,309 --> 00:07:17,290
[SPEAKER_00]: You already know that.

113
00:07:17,510 --> 00:07:18,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody knows that.

114
00:07:19,492 --> 00:07:19,992
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

115
00:07:20,012 --> 00:07:28,080
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not overnight you will be celibate and never eat again, but you need to adopt that as a value, that your desires need to be restrained.

116
00:07:28,581 --> 00:07:33,144
[SPEAKER_00]: And your desires are not rooted in these random things in beautiful sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

117
00:07:33,165 --> 00:07:34,866
[SPEAKER_00]: They're rooted in your own intention.

118
00:07:36,747 --> 00:07:38,529
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you will learn where the problem is.

119
00:07:38,569 --> 00:07:43,933
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, man's sensuality is not the beautiful things in the world that he can see or taste or touch.

120
00:07:44,493 --> 00:07:45,214
[SPEAKER_00]: It's his thoughts.

121
00:07:46,014 --> 00:07:47,315
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where sensuality is rooted.

122
00:07:48,076 --> 00:07:53,680
[SPEAKER_00]: But you'll never get to see the roots of that sensuality if you never restrain your own actions and your own senses.

123
00:07:56,438 --> 00:07:57,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's again exactly another thing.

124
00:07:57,979 --> 00:08:04,103
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to wait until it's too late, until your senses start decaying and so on, in order to practice that.

125
00:08:04,243 --> 00:08:06,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, if you wait, then it will be too late.

126
00:08:13,589 --> 00:08:25,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you did not, if you could not restrain your senses while they were still relatively young and operating normally, basically, while the domains of those senses were clear to you,

127
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:29,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Sights were clear, pleasures for the sights were clear.

128
00:08:30,321 --> 00:08:33,261
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not gonna restrain it when it's all gone, basically.

129
00:08:33,361 --> 00:08:39,742
[SPEAKER_00]: When you become senile and you can't see well, you can't hear well, everything is uncomfortable, everything is just too much weight on you.

130
00:08:41,303 --> 00:08:45,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you had an option to exercise strength and develop strength, you didn't.

131
00:08:46,583 --> 00:08:47,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you can.

132
00:08:48,024 --> 00:08:48,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Now it's...

133
00:08:49,104 --> 00:08:52,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the bare minimum that's required for you to start developing strength, it's too much.

134
00:08:53,845 --> 00:08:54,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you waited too long.

135
00:08:56,966 --> 00:08:59,907
[SPEAKER_00]: The same for health, life, anything.

136
00:09:00,668 --> 00:09:02,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you don't want to wait till it's too long.

137
00:09:06,090 --> 00:09:23,897
[SPEAKER_00]: And if sometimes you get complacent, like, because, you know, sometimes there's nothing bothering you, you're kind of practicing, so you don't feel necessarily guilty that you're lazy or procrastinating, but, you know, you are getting complacent, you maybe have trouble seeing where the problem is, well, just maybe turn up your restraint or not.

138
00:09:25,167 --> 00:09:25,967
[SPEAKER_00]: And the pain will come.

139
00:09:26,708 --> 00:09:27,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's your work.

140
00:09:28,509 --> 00:09:29,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, am I resisting this pain?

141
00:09:30,290 --> 00:09:31,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I fearing this pain?

142
00:09:32,091 --> 00:09:34,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I trying to justify my actions to get rid of this pain?

143
00:09:34,912 --> 00:09:36,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Or am I genuinely in war?

144
00:09:36,413 --> 00:09:38,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Because this is not a pain that harms me, by the way.

145
00:09:39,515 --> 00:09:44,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no to smell, sight, smell, taste, touches, doesn't harm you at all.

146
00:09:44,817 --> 00:09:51,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, there is no threat, no danger in saying no to not look at something, in saying no to not taste something.

147
00:09:52,110 --> 00:09:53,371
[SPEAKER_00]: There is really nothing dangerous.

148
00:09:53,611 --> 00:09:55,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, it's the worst type of pain.

149
00:09:55,592 --> 00:09:56,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

150
00:09:56,193 --> 00:10:00,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because the mind and the senses are used to going wherever desire wants to go.

151
00:10:01,216 --> 00:10:02,777
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like catching a wild dog.

152
00:10:03,478 --> 00:10:09,762
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be kicking and screaming and trying to bite your face off, if you tie down first time.

153
00:10:11,043 --> 00:10:13,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in its mind, it's fighting for its life.

154
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:15,040
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's actually safe.

155
00:10:15,341 --> 00:10:18,382
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually exactly, yeah, this is the... yeah, that's the safety.

156
00:10:18,422 --> 00:10:19,583
[SPEAKER_00]: But he doesn't recognize it's such.

157
00:10:19,923 --> 00:10:22,064
[SPEAKER_00]: But once he calms down, then he gets to recognize it.

158
00:10:22,544 --> 00:10:26,486
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, once it's completely tame, then it's tame, then it's fixed.

159
00:10:28,850 --> 00:10:43,400
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the Buddha compared your own mind and senses exactly always to wild animals, to the big independent bull elephant that you have to catch and then strap down with the big sort of chains and leather belts until it... basically until it calms down.

160
00:10:44,261 --> 00:10:45,522
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it will listen to you.

161
00:10:45,922 --> 00:10:46,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

162
00:10:46,903 --> 00:10:51,746
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to restrain your senses and then eventually they will listen to you, not you to them.

163
00:10:57,217 --> 00:11:03,821
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, sometimes these things like people can get reminders of, oh, health is a, you know, a precarious thing.

164
00:11:03,941 --> 00:11:04,901
[SPEAKER_00]: It can quickly change.

165
00:11:05,281 --> 00:11:06,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Life is precarious thing.

166
00:11:06,342 --> 00:11:07,062
[SPEAKER_00]: It can quickly change.

167
00:11:07,643 --> 00:11:13,546
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever were to inspire you to practice in the right direction, that have the right type of contemplation, that's fine.

168
00:11:13,886 --> 00:11:14,286
[SPEAKER_00]: That's good.

169
00:11:15,267 --> 00:11:15,607
[SPEAKER_00]: But...

170
00:11:17,302 --> 00:11:22,403
[SPEAKER_00]: You can also find that inspiration within yourself and not wait to be prompted externally.

171
00:11:22,903 --> 00:11:26,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Because more often than not, the external prompts means it's too late.

172
00:11:26,964 --> 00:11:30,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Like there, there were some cases in the suttas who had external prompts.

173
00:11:30,925 --> 00:11:35,365
[SPEAKER_00]: As a result of that, they made the effort and purified their mind completely.

174
00:11:35,946 --> 00:11:41,027
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think those cases are far fewer in number, which is why we still talk about them today.

175
00:11:42,427 --> 00:11:43,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Two millennia later.

176
00:11:45,297 --> 00:11:50,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, they were so profound, you know, the inspirational stories, that that's why you still remember them.

177
00:11:51,362 --> 00:11:55,265
[SPEAKER_00]: But far fewer than the actual... the ones they were not.

178
00:11:55,445 --> 00:12:00,409
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, they were facing these adverse circumstances and had no direction of practice.

179
00:12:02,171 --> 00:12:07,835
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, as you're saying, you're there, like, foresight is wisdom.

180
00:12:08,256 --> 00:12:09,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Foresight is wisdom, yeah.

181
00:12:10,097 --> 00:12:11,859
[SPEAKER_01]: That is, having a perspective on...

182
00:12:12,299 --> 00:12:12,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

183
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:14,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Having a better, greater perspective.

184
00:12:16,860 --> 00:12:17,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Elevated ground.

185
00:12:18,502 --> 00:12:26,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you find a way to climb onto the elevated ground and you see perspective is farther reaching from here than if I come down.

186
00:12:27,690 --> 00:12:28,431
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I have an option.

187
00:12:28,451 --> 00:12:38,060
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll either come down and enjoy whatever fruit grows at the bottom of this hill, but I won't see any danger coming, because I've been toxicating, chasing down berries and apples.

188
00:12:39,782 --> 00:12:41,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I can perhaps, you know,

189
00:12:42,413 --> 00:12:58,486
[SPEAKER_00]: I have my expectations in terms of providing objects for my sense desires, but then stay in the place of safety, from which I can see the danger coming from far, far, far beyond.

190
00:13:02,489 --> 00:13:05,172
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all within, like within everyone's mind.

191
00:13:05,892 --> 00:13:08,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Like even now, yeah, I might not see the end of this day.

192
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:17,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if nothing happens to you, your heart might stop, as we said that before, in those talks on maraṇasati.

193
00:13:20,007 --> 00:13:20,968
[SPEAKER_00]: So you realize, okay, so...

194
00:13:21,988 --> 00:13:22,909
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to... so what do I do?

195
00:13:22,929 --> 00:13:25,892
[SPEAKER_00]: What you do is, don't lose perspective that your heart might stop by the end of the day.

196
00:13:27,953 --> 00:13:32,357
[SPEAKER_00]: And all you need to do is protect that perspective, everything else will come out of it, naturally.

197
00:13:33,858 --> 00:13:35,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-desire will come out of it.

198
00:13:35,420 --> 00:13:36,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-hatred will come out of it.

199
00:13:37,081 --> 00:13:38,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-distraction will come out of it.

200
00:13:38,883 --> 00:13:39,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Peace will come out of it.

201
00:13:40,747 --> 00:13:46,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only reason people don't live in peace is because they keep disturbing themselves by allowing their own senses to drag them left and right.

202
00:13:47,488 --> 00:13:49,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, imagine you get a big dog on a leash.

203
00:13:49,829 --> 00:13:50,549
[SPEAKER_00]: It's stronger than you.

204
00:13:51,949 --> 00:13:54,449
[SPEAKER_00]: And it sees a rabbit or a squirrel.

205
00:13:56,210 --> 00:13:56,930
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're tied to it.

206
00:13:58,590 --> 00:13:59,490
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be very painful.

207
00:14:01,491 --> 00:14:09,152
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you have... doesn't matter how big the dog is, if it's trained, and if at the slightest sort of tightness of the leash it will stop, that's it.

208
00:14:09,672 --> 00:14:10,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Here it is.

209
00:14:12,492 --> 00:14:13,192
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not threatened.

210
00:14:13,732 --> 00:14:36,319
[SPEAKER_00]: And many people know implicitly that if circumstances were to change and they were to be pushed to the limits due to circumstances, due to sensuality, ill-will or something happening, they have implicit sort of recognition of the fear that they will completely lose control.

211
00:14:38,580 --> 00:14:39,620
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's frightening.

212
00:14:40,998 --> 00:14:42,778
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no idea where you're gonna end up.

213
00:14:44,379 --> 00:14:49,520
[SPEAKER_00]: And sometimes people, yeah, end up where they don't want to end up, on account of just careless decisions and choices.

214
00:14:52,161 --> 00:14:57,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And all of that is rooted in not having that fundamental perspective on your own situation.

215
00:14:57,822 --> 00:15:03,804
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is rooted in presently, basically, enduring intoxication.

216
00:15:04,024 --> 00:15:06,824
[SPEAKER_00]: That's, that's the, like, a basis of the mind.

217
00:15:06,844 --> 00:15:09,285
[SPEAKER_00]: You think you will live forever, you will be healthy forever,

218
00:15:10,312 --> 00:15:19,137
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will be sort of... your senses will be operating properly, strongly, clearly, forever.

219
00:15:19,758 --> 00:15:22,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you'll be... you're never gonna age, never gonna go decrepit.

220
00:15:37,787 --> 00:15:53,041
[SPEAKER_00]: And these little reminders of the perspective would be much more apparent and much more self-evident if people would spend their most of the time within the renunciation and sense restraint, not within sensuality and desire.

221
00:15:54,382 --> 00:15:55,343
[SPEAKER_00]: You lose perspective.

222
00:15:55,383 --> 00:16:00,288
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha called, basically, sensuality and other hindrances, the weakness of wisdom.

223
00:16:01,504 --> 00:16:07,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as we compare the wisdom to that elevated ground from which you have a better perspective, well, this is literally dragging you down.

224
00:16:08,432 --> 00:16:09,253
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep coming down.

225
00:16:09,273 --> 00:16:10,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, you lose perspective.

226
00:16:12,016 --> 00:16:15,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, if you constantly live on the lower planes, you'll never get the perspective.

227
00:16:17,252 --> 00:16:21,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Or if you had it, you lost it completely, so you need to retrace your steps and go back up.

228
00:16:21,960 --> 00:16:31,395
[SPEAKER_00]: But what I'm saying is people don't go back up, do not go back to the wiser perspective, because they keep choosing centrality, choosing desire, choosing scratch and itch.

229
00:16:32,297 --> 00:16:35,799
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't choose it, you're automatically climbing up the hill on a higher ground.

230
00:16:36,259 --> 00:16:45,964
[SPEAKER_01]: But then there's this idea that by sort of teasing those hindrances, you learn to, you know, you're challenging those hindrances.

231
00:16:45,984 --> 00:16:47,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Teasing the hindrances?

232
00:16:47,124 --> 00:16:48,525
[SPEAKER_01]: You're practicing with them.

233
00:16:48,545 --> 00:16:51,066
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's like you're teasing a wild crocodile.

234
00:16:51,307 --> 00:16:51,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

235
00:16:51,527 --> 00:16:52,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Teasing a wild hyena.

236
00:16:52,967 --> 00:16:53,988
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you get stronger.

237
00:16:54,950 --> 00:16:55,850
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the result of that?

238
00:16:55,950 --> 00:16:59,151
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just gonna basically get you in worse when it gets you.

239
00:16:59,231 --> 00:17:01,232
[SPEAKER_01]: You're in a battle.

240
00:17:01,432 --> 00:17:01,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

241
00:17:01,912 --> 00:17:04,593
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't train a dog by teasing it to be wild.

242
00:17:05,113 --> 00:17:06,613
[SPEAKER_00]: You train it by calming it down.

243
00:17:07,393 --> 00:17:10,754
[SPEAKER_00]: So, only person basically... You know who would say you tease it?

244
00:17:11,054 --> 00:17:12,615
[SPEAKER_00]: The one who couldn't calm it down.

245
00:17:13,255 --> 00:17:17,038
[SPEAKER_00]: It was too weak to calm an animal down, and then we try to make its own way.

246
00:17:17,538 --> 00:17:18,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no, no, no, no.

247
00:17:18,118 --> 00:17:19,899
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not supposed to calm your senses down.

248
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:23,001
[SPEAKER_00]: You're supposed to tease them out, because there's nothing else that I can do.

249
00:17:23,021 --> 00:17:23,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no.

250
00:17:23,182 --> 00:17:24,642
[SPEAKER_01]: You're supposed to run with them.

251
00:17:24,903 --> 00:17:25,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Run with them?

252
00:17:25,703 --> 00:17:26,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

253
00:17:26,504 --> 00:17:28,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Run alongside the wild dog.

254
00:17:29,286 --> 00:17:29,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, wow.

255
00:17:30,426 --> 00:17:31,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, why don't you try that?

256
00:17:31,827 --> 00:17:33,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, actually get a wild dog.

257
00:17:33,808 --> 00:17:35,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then you've...

258
00:17:35,349 --> 00:17:40,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Tie yourself to it, and try running, and see how far you get, before you end up being dragged against your will.

259
00:17:41,636 --> 00:17:51,085
[SPEAKER_00]: And if that's going to be that hard for a wild dog, random wild animal, imagine how bad it's going to be if you do that to your own senses.

260
00:17:51,205 --> 00:17:54,368
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, it's based on a presumption that you can run.

261
00:17:56,930 --> 00:17:59,613
[SPEAKER_00]: What if you can't run anymore?

262
00:17:59,833 --> 00:18:00,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the thing.

263
00:18:00,714 --> 00:18:07,320
[SPEAKER_00]: You know when people depend on constantly having something to do, activity, engagement, projects, give meaning to their life.

264
00:18:08,975 --> 00:18:10,116
[SPEAKER_00]: It basically helps them float.

265
00:18:10,816 --> 00:18:16,961
[SPEAKER_00]: And if they are left alone, even for half a day, without company, without engagement, without talking, they start losing their mind.

266
00:18:18,122 --> 00:18:21,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, now there is nothing to run along with, to keep you occupied.

267
00:18:22,545 --> 00:18:26,608
[SPEAKER_00]: You are left with the nature of that thing and you realize, oh my goodness, this is terrifying.

268
00:18:27,108 --> 00:18:28,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it can rip you apart.

269
00:18:28,509 --> 00:18:32,112
[SPEAKER_00]: If one animal runs to the left, the other animal runs to the right,

270
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:33,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one do you want to follow?

271
00:18:34,060 --> 00:18:35,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have six animals, by the way.

272
00:18:36,421 --> 00:18:37,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Six senses.

273
00:18:37,221 --> 00:18:38,241
[SPEAKER_00]: So, which one do you run with?

274
00:18:38,981 --> 00:18:39,601
[SPEAKER_00]: All six of them?

275
00:18:39,661 --> 00:18:40,381
[SPEAKER_00]: How's that possible?

276
00:18:41,722 --> 00:18:43,622
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to live with the wild animals or...?

277
00:18:44,262 --> 00:18:48,483
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to live with the wild animals and at the same time pretend they are not wild.

278
00:18:49,363 --> 00:18:58,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, close yourself in the room with a crocodile, hyena, wild macaques or any chimpanzee or something and see how well that will go.

279
00:18:58,804 --> 00:19:01,705
[SPEAKER_01]: See how peaceful it... How peaceful that will be, yeah.

280
00:19:03,268 --> 00:19:04,669
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's why it's not optional.

281
00:19:05,189 --> 00:19:06,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Calming your senses.

282
00:19:06,870 --> 00:19:07,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Restraining your senses.

283
00:19:08,011 --> 00:19:13,374
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, I can do away with this, because I got this magic, magic technique for my meditation.

284
00:19:13,774 --> 00:19:13,934
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

285
00:19:14,375 --> 00:19:16,016
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no meditation without sense restraint.

286
00:19:16,596 --> 00:19:18,717
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to start meditation, you start sense restraint.

287
00:19:18,737 --> 00:19:19,998
[SPEAKER_00]: That is your meditation.

288
00:19:20,478 --> 00:19:21,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Meditation will come out of it.

289
00:19:22,420 --> 00:19:24,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Once the animals come, like these ones, and sit next to you.

290
00:19:25,622 --> 00:19:27,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're in charge of them, they're not in charge of you.

291
00:19:29,804 --> 00:19:32,105
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's your own senses.

292
00:19:32,125 --> 00:19:33,006
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I will not look at that.

293
00:19:34,368 --> 00:19:35,329
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I will not say that.

294
00:19:36,190 --> 00:19:37,831
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I will not go after that sense of touch.

295
00:19:39,933 --> 00:19:40,654
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unpleasant, yes.

296
00:19:41,214 --> 00:19:52,785
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I choose this discomfort on account of far worse discomfort of a rabid mind being dragged left and right by those same senses that you were trying to appease.

297
00:19:53,106 --> 00:19:53,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's good.

298
00:19:54,327 --> 00:19:55,027
[SPEAKER_01]: It's good for me.

299
00:19:55,248 --> 00:19:56,128
[SPEAKER_01]: It's good to have...

300
00:19:56,148 --> 00:19:56,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

301
00:19:56,689 --> 00:19:58,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you want to do what's good for you.

302
00:19:59,291 --> 00:19:59,692
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just...

303
00:20:00,622 --> 00:20:06,943
[SPEAKER_00]: One person acts out of no real perspective on what's really good for you, and then another person develops perspective on what's really good for me.

304
00:20:07,583 --> 00:20:13,265
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, actually, feeding the wild animal when it's in its fit of madness, it's not good for me.

305
00:20:14,285 --> 00:20:20,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, it calms it down for a second, but makes it even wilder, because now it associates that wild behavior with reward.

306
00:20:22,486 --> 00:20:24,187
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't reward the wild behavior.

307
00:20:24,607 --> 00:20:25,907
[SPEAKER_00]: I reward peaceful behavior.

308
00:20:27,367 --> 00:20:29,528
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how... that's how to train yourself.

309
00:20:36,131 --> 00:20:38,452
[SPEAKER_01]: You reward peaceful behavior.

310
00:20:38,472 --> 00:20:39,153
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the reward?

311
00:20:40,413 --> 00:20:41,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's that, basically, recognition.

312
00:20:44,035 --> 00:20:46,616
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, are we still talking about the simile or about you, yourself?

313
00:20:46,796 --> 00:20:48,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, slightly different.

314
00:20:48,277 --> 00:20:48,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

315
00:20:48,617 --> 00:20:50,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, it's like reward...

316
00:20:52,599 --> 00:20:58,422
[SPEAKER_00]: In your own case, say, in terms of the sense restraint, the reward is absence of desire now.

317
00:20:58,442 --> 00:21:01,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you keep increasing the desire, in the same sense you would increase the

318
00:21:02,956 --> 00:21:09,518
[SPEAKER_00]: The madness of an animal, by scratching it, by teasing it, by feeding it, by giving it more, when it wants more.

319
00:21:11,038 --> 00:21:14,079
[SPEAKER_00]: So, initially, it hurts to say no, but after a while, desire disappears.

320
00:21:15,519 --> 00:21:18,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you stop scratching your itch, you stop it, like freeing yourself from addiction.

321
00:21:19,220 --> 00:21:26,862
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be going back to that which you were addicted, when you're free from addiction, when there's no desire towards it, and then you recognize that whole relief.

322
00:21:27,302 --> 00:21:32,203
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm free from that big pressure of that desire, because I endured away from it.

323
00:21:33,748 --> 00:21:35,290
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you won't go back to it.

324
00:21:36,070 --> 00:21:52,868
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you will, if you completely lose wisdom again, if you completely lose perspective, if you completely stop recognizing that that's much better for you, and much more pleasurable, actually, to not engage in that wild, mad addiction, whatever else, craving, desire, satisfaction, and so on.

325
00:21:54,170 --> 00:21:58,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the absence of that pressure is pleasant.

326
00:21:59,814 --> 00:22:01,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just that it's pleasant.

327
00:22:01,875 --> 00:22:06,759
[SPEAKER_00]: The absence of that pressure is the only reason you give in to the pressure, because you want it to go away.

328
00:22:07,699 --> 00:22:13,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you found a direct way that doesn't involve giving in, that doesn't involve being dragged around by a wild animal.

329
00:22:13,423 --> 00:22:18,507
[SPEAKER_00]: You found a direct way of removing the pressure, basically deflating it, depressuring it.

330
00:22:19,871 --> 00:22:20,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Renunciation.

331
00:22:21,172 --> 00:22:25,096
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the pleasure renunciation that's loftier than any other pleasure you can experience in this life.

332
00:22:25,116 --> 00:22:26,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you have too much of that?

333
00:22:26,797 --> 00:22:26,958
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

334
00:22:27,778 --> 00:22:30,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that pleasure, as the Buddha said, pleasure of jhāna, basically, that's what we're talking about.

335
00:22:31,582 --> 00:22:35,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure of renunciation, which is the pleasure of jhāna, you can't... there is no craving for it.

336
00:22:36,286 --> 00:22:40,510
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the result of you saying no to your craving, sufficiently long enough.

337
00:22:41,791 --> 00:22:42,932
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then... and the Buddha realized.

338
00:22:42,952 --> 00:22:45,475
[SPEAKER_00]: He asked himself, oh, is this... why am I afraid of this pleasure?

339
00:22:46,460 --> 00:22:47,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Should I be afraid of these pleasures?

340
00:22:47,860 --> 00:22:48,021
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

341
00:22:48,521 --> 00:22:51,142
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing unwholesome underlying this pleasure.

342
00:22:51,822 --> 00:22:52,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I won't be afraid of it.

343
00:22:53,622 --> 00:22:54,243
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll cultivate it.

344
00:22:54,263 --> 00:22:59,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you cultivate pleasure of renunciation, at the same time, you cannot cultivate pleasure towards sensuality.

345
00:22:59,764 --> 00:23:13,029
[SPEAKER_00]: If you cultivate pain of renunciation, pain of elements, pain of withdrawal, you cannot have the pain of household life, pain of losing your children, pain of losing your wealth, pain of, like, like, whatever, abuse, anything else that people can suffer and encounter.

346
00:23:13,509 --> 00:23:14,329
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't apply to you.

347
00:23:16,353 --> 00:23:21,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind is already far, far withdrawn from that domain.

